        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly present Goneril's love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding comparisons amplify the asserted degree of that love; possible insincerity does not reduce its avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence answers Lear's question about which daughter presently loves him most. Neither the focal words nor Goneril's surrounding speech position her as undertaking, renewing, or invoking a specific duty toward him. Her comparison with how a child loves a father describes the claimed magnitude of feeling rather than affirming herself as bound to future care or obedience. Lear's grant of territory is a separate responsive act, not a commitment made by Goneril.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an extended, carefully structured answer to a formal solicitation, not as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its emotional language remains part of a controlled avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression substantially functions as competitive praise or flattery offered in a public contest for Lear's bounty. Lear explicitly asks for a comparison of the daughters' love and ties the answer to the size of his gift, while Goneril answers with superlative rhetoric.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and links the answer to his bounty. Goneril, speaking first, gives an elaborate declaration of unsurpassable filial love. Lear accepts the performance and immediately grants her territory.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established for this occurrence. It is received by Lear as a successful declaration in the love contest, and he rewards it with a territorial grant. Later supplied discourse strongly challenges the declaration's sincerity and characterizes the sisters' speeches as flattery or cunning, but that does not convert the original avowal into a promise or erase its truth-presenting force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly supports interpreting the speech as strategic flattery, but the classification concerns the marked occurrence's represented force rather than conclusively determining Goneril's entire inner emotional state. Lear's subsequent grant and Goneril's later conduct are contextual evidence, not words contained in the focal expression.\"}"
        }
